From be70fc4f34565c1ed2ef717f11990d8bee0404e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 09:26:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 180/267] media: uapi: add MPEG-4 Part 2 and H.263 stateless
 decoder support

Add the V4L2_PIX_FMT_MPEG4_SLICE and V4L2_PIX_FMT_H263_SLICE pixel
formats and the compound controls needed to drive stateless MPEG-4
Part 2 decoders (ISO/IEC 14496-2 Simple and Advanced Simple profiles)
and H.263 decoders:

  V4L2_CID_STATELESS_MPEG4_VOL           Video Object Layer header
  V4L2_CID_STATELESS_MPEG4_VOP           Video Object Plane header
  V4L2_CID_STATELESS_MPEG4_QUANTISATION  quantisation matrices

The API is frame based: one request decodes one coded VOP, which the
output buffer holds from its start code to its end, including the
resynchronisation markers of its video packets. As for MPEG-2, the
reference pictures are designated by the timestamps of their capture
buffers and the quantisation matrices default to those of the
specification. VOPs which are not coded are not queued.

MPEG-4 Part 2 specifies H.263 baseline as its short video header mode,
so both pixel formats share the controls: an H.263 picture is described
with V4L2_MPEG4_VOL_FLAG_SHORT_VIDEO_HEADER set and with the
few picture header fields which have no MPEG-4 equivalent. H.263 has
a pixel format of its own so that decoders can advertise it separately.
Baseline is what a driver which exposes it has to decode. The other
modes of the H.263 picture header (extended picture type, custom
picture format, the optional coding modes of the annexes) are passed as
flags, so that a driver can reject from the controls alone what it
cannot decode.

MPEG-4 decoders differ in how much of the VOP header they parse. The
VOP control therefore carries the complete header state, including the
sprite trajectory of S(GMC)-VOPs and the temporal distances which the
direct mode of B-VOPs needs, and the size of the header so that the
hardware can be started on the first macroblock. It also carries the
coding types of the reference VOPs, which the decoding of B-VOPs
depends on, so that a driver does not have to remember them from
earlier requests. Optional tools which many decoders lack (global
motion compensation, quarter sample motion compensation, data
partitioning) are signalled by the VOL control, which drivers reject if
they do not support the stream.

The existing V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_MPEG4_PROFILE and
V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_MPEG4_LEVEL controls can be used to enumerate the
supported profiles and levels.

